<BaseLayout title="Why GitOps — Ilhaam Ghiffari" description="Declarative state, self-healing clusters, and the audit trail GitOps buys you.">
  <article class="prose">
    <h1>Why GitOps</h1>
    <p class="sub">Aug 2026 · ArgoCD on this very cluster</p>

    <p>
      The classic deploy is imperative: you <em>tell</em> the cluster what to do
      (<code>kubectl apply</code>, <code>helm upgrade</code>, <code>az aks ...</code>). GitOps flips
      it: the cluster <em>watches</em> git and converges itself. Your desired state lives in a
      repository; an operator makes reality match it.
    </p>

    <h2>What it actually buys you</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>One source of truth.</strong> The manifests in <code>k8s/</code> are the spec.
        Nobody can drift the cluster by hand — if they try, ArgoCD's self-heal reverts it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>An audit trail by default.</strong> Every change to the cluster is a commit:
        who, what, when, and why (the commit message). This site's deploys are literally
        <code>chore: bump image to &lt;sha&gt; [skip ci]</code> commits made by the pipeline.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Rollback is a revert.</strong> The app misbehaves after a release? Revert the
        commit. The cluster converges back to the previous state — no restore-from-backup dance.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Review before change.</strong> Since everything is a PR-able file, cluster changes
        get the same review process as application code.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How it looks here</h2>
    <p>
      ArgoCD runs an Application whose source is this repo's <code>k8s/</code> directory
      (<code>directory.recurse: true</code> — learned that the hard way), with automated sync,
      prune, and self-heal. CI commits the new image tag; ArgoCD detects the drift and rolls it
      out. The ingress, network policies, cert-manager issuer, and the ServiceMonitor that feeds
      Prometheus are all the same kind of file — security and observability as code, deployed by
      the same loop as the app.
    </p>

    <h2>When GitOps is not the answer</h2>
    <p>
      Honest caveat: GitOps is a poor fit for stateful hot paths (databases), for truly
      time-critical rollouts, and it adds machinery that's overkill for a single static site. The
      real payoff shows when a team shares a platform and needs review, auditability, and
      convergence — which is exactly the internal-developer-platform story this repo is practicing
      at portfolio scale.
    </p>

